/// zenohd — the single First Motive router, on the always-on office machine.
///
/// Every bridge and every client connects here in `client` mode, so this process
/// is the one place the whole fleet meets. Nothing else runs a router: a second
/// one would partition the fleet or duplicate every sample.
///
/// The router does NOT live on the GPU workstation. That machine is wiped,
/// rebooted, and loaded with sim and inference, and every reboot takes the whole
/// fleet's discovery point with it. It lives on the office Mac mini, which is
/// always on, wired, and on the tailnet — and it runs on that machine's HOST,
/// under launchd, never inside its CI guest. The guest is ephemeral and network
/// isolated by design; a router there is a router that disappears.
///
/// This file is a TEMPLATE. `scripts/install-router.sh` renders it and writes the
/// result to /etc/fm-comms/router.json5. Committed files carry placeholders only —
/// never a real address. Read the render before installing it:
///
///     ./run.sh render router
///
/// Placeholders:
///   FM_ROUTER_LISTEN      the JSON array of endpoints zenohd binds — the host's
///                         LAN address and its tailnet address, both as
///                         tcp/<ip>:<port>. Resolved on the host at render time
///                         from the LAN interface (FM_ROUTER_LAN_IF, else the
///                         default route) and `tailscale ip -4`, so neither
///                         address is ever written into a tracked file. Force a
///                         single one with FM_ROUTER_BIND_IP on a bench host.
{
  mode: "router",

  /// Two endpoints, and exactly two: the LAN address and the tailnet address.
  ///
  /// In the office a rig connects over plain TCP on the switch it is already on;
  /// off-site, or on a Wi-Fi link that filters multicast, the same rig connects
  /// through the tailnet. Tailnet-only would put the Jetson's compressed camera
  /// streams through WireGuard on its own CPU while it sits one port away, for no
  /// gain. A rig that moves changes its endpoint, not its transport.
  ///
  /// Binding every interface is still refused. A wildcard would offer the fleet's
  /// whole topic graph to anything that can reach the box — on the Mac mini, its
  /// CI guest network included. The office LAN is a place we choose to be
  /// reachable; the guest network is not. The tailnet remains the authenticated
  /// perimeter for everything off-site, which is what lets this run without
  /// TLS/mTLS. See docs/REMOTE.md for how a contractor is granted router:7447 and
  /// nothing else.
  listen: {
    endpoints: ${FM_ROUTER_LISTEN},
  },

  /// The fleet is explicit: bridges and clients are configured with this router's
  /// endpoint. Multicast scouting would additionally pull in whatever happens to
  /// be on the office LAN, which is exactly the accidental-peering problem the
  /// DDS setup already had. Gossip stays on so a client learns peers through the
  /// router it is already connected to.
  scouting: {
    multicast: { enabled: false },
    gossip: { enabled: true },
  },

  transport: {
    link: {
      /// A rig on Wi-Fi drops out; without a keepalive the router holds a dead
      /// session and the desktop shows a live-looking rig that never updates.
      tx: { keep_alive: 4 },
      rx: {
        /// Camera frames are the largest samples on the wire. The 10 MB ceiling
        /// covers a compressed frame with headroom; raw frames are denied at the
        /// bridge rather than accommodated here.
        max_message_size: 10485760,
      },
    },
  },
}
